//! Entries slice -- Batch 145 (Plan 130 / Research 171) -- the Metal-shape
//! pack. Two `kernel_opt` rules from two staged leads (the Batch-145 charter,
//! `.distill/001`): `direct-low-bit-generation-replaces-exp-quantize-roundtrip`
//! (B +1, Compute -- the EFQ-Softmax paper lane, arXiv 2609.09721, walk #9
//! lead 1) and `kv-split-decode-underfill-per-slot-validity` (F +1, flash-attention
//! Tiling -- the MTPLX PR #391 lane, youssofal/MTPLX @ `a5e38bb7` Apache-2.0,
//! MLX 0.32.2). Plus 1 suggest-only `rust_perf` rule (`mincore-probe-routes-
//! gather-arm`, `entries_06.rs`) and 2 evidence riders (the underfill negative
//! cell is DUAL-SOURCED in the KV-split rule's regime note -- MTPLX `hyper_read`
//! v1 + Hopper 2609.12923 fixed 64-row GMMA fragments, never a standalone
//! rule; the hyper-read fusion rider lands on the same-input-gemv family,
//! `entries_gpu_07.rs` -- dedup-OVERLAPPED, never standalone). Corpus 612 ->
//! 614 (B 63 -> 64, F 14 -> 15).
//!
//! DEDUP CORRECTION AT ADMISSION (the probe-first law, again): the charter's
//! "split-K dedup CLEAN -- no attention split-K rule exists" was WRONG --
//! `split-k-two-pass-attention-decode-long-context` (Batch 105,
//! `entries_gpu_18.rs`) already covers the dense long-context two-pass split,
//! and its Batch-142 rider already carries an occupancy-deficit trigger
//! ("split-KV fires as an OCCUPANCY patch, not a length threshold alone").
//! The earlier `split.?k` grep was truncated before the rule-id list. This
//! batch's rule is therefore scoped to the genuinely-new delta: the
//! WIDTH-UNDERFILL trigger (rows x heads < SM count at decode M -- not a
//! length crossover) + the SPARSE per-slot-validity half (unsorted selection,
//! prefix-cut double-count hazard), with a loud distinct-from row + a
//! reciprocal cross-ref rider on the Batch-105 rule.
//!
//! Quote discipline: two independent quote lanes (EFQ paper lane + MTPLX
//! clone lane at the pinned SHA) + caller spot-checks of every load-bearing
//! quote (verbatim). Paper-lane corrections carried: the -40.33% figure is
//! the VECTOR STAGE of the fused probability-generation kernel only
//! (fixpipe 0.3-1.0%, mmad identical by construction and excluded) and the
//! kernel table is labeled "LUT" -- the variant ambiguity is carried, never
//! flattened; the quality claim is "within 0.005 of FP16 while beating
//! MXFP4" (the language-task gain is operating-point-dependent: +0.0024 at
//! batch 8, +0.0002 at batch 16; the large gains are VL grounding). No
//! "Apple" attribution -- the paper says only "the A5 vector unit".
//! MTPLX-lane correction carried: the 13.2 vs 67.8 tok/s negative cell is
//! `hyper_read` v1's (used in-tree as the general underfill negative), and the
//! QSA-specific motivation arithmetic is "EIGHT threadgroups at M=4" --
//! both quoted with their own hardware contexts (M4 verifier vs M5 Max
//! comment), never merged into one claim.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ============================================================
        // -- direct-low-bit-generation-replaces-exp-quantize-roundtrip --
        // Batch 145 / Research 171 / Plan 130 (EFQ lane).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "direct-low-bit-generation-replaces-exp-quantize-roundtrip"
                .to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "In a FlashAttention-style kernel whose low-bit P.V matmul consumes a QUANTIZED probability tile, do not materialize probabilities in wide precision and then quantize -- the exp-then-quantize roundtrip pays a dense elementwise exp PLUS a separate quantize stage and rounds twice. Generate the low-bit codes DIRECTLY from the row-max-shifted scores: per microscaling block take the block max M_b, pick the EXPONENT-ONLY scale s_b = 2^k_b with k_b = floor((M_b + log(2/9))/log 2) (the constant centers the block under the E2M1 max code 6 -- it is part of the scale rule, not a fitted parameter), map each score to the residual log domain z_t = x_t - k_b*log2 - log 6, and emit the code with ONE calibrated affine rule c_t = clip_[0,7](floor((z_t - tau)*h) + 1); feed the SAME (code, scale) operand to BOTH the P~.V numerator update and the P~.1 denominator update -- 'If the numerator used EFQ-Softmax but the denominator used a separately reconstructed probability block, the online update would combine two different probability approximations.' Row-max update, the historical rescale alpha (ONE row-level exp retained -- 'Exp-Free' refers to removing the dense elementwise exponential for the current score block, not every exponential), accumulation width, and the final division are unchanged. Measured: 'EFQ-Softmax reduces the vector-stage latency of the fused probability-generation kernel by 40.33% on average across sequence lengths from 16K to 128K' on the paper's A5 vector unit (fixpipe gains only 0.3-1.0%; the mmad stage is identical by construction and EXCLUDED -- so the end-to-end win is bounded by the vector-stage share of the kernel; the paper's kernel table is labeled 'LUT', i.e. the fused-LUT ablation variant, so quote the figure as the fused probability-generation kernel's, never as end-to-end attention). Quality: Qwen3-8B 7-task mean 0.6749 (MXFP4) -> 0.6773, 'both 8-code operating points stay within 0.005 of the FP16 baseline (0.6803)'; the large gains are VL grounding (Qwen3-VL 9-task mean 0.7826 -> 0.8000) -- the language gain is operating-point-dependent (+0.0024 at batch 8, +0.0002 at batch 16), so the durable claim is beats-MXFP4-within-0.005-of-FP16, not uniform improvement. Regime: applies when (a) the consumer is a BLOCK-SCALED FP4/FP8 matmul whose microscaling block matches the probability operand layout ('the implementation keeps B consistent with the low-bit matrix operand layout'), (b) scores are row-max-shifted so x_t <= 0 (the nonnegative code set C_8 = {0, 1/2, 1, 3/2, 2, 3, 4, 6} is the whole story), (c) numerator and denominator can share one operand, and (d) a one-time OFFLINE calibration of (tau, h) against OUTPUT-level attention error is acceptable -- values are model/domain-specific ((-2.90, 2.00) MMLU-tuned, (-3.06, 2.30) mean-tuned, (-2.10, 2.70) VL-tuned), fixed at inference, never stored per token/row/block. Does NOT apply when exact scalar probability values are the correctness target ('The correctness target is not exact scalar exponential reconstruction... This design trades scalar nearest-rounding optimality for a simpler probability-code generation path' -- the affine thresholds are uniform in the log domain where nearest-rounding of E2M1 would be nonuniform); when there is no block-scaled low-bit consumer (the exponent-only scale rule exists to center the block under the E2M1 max code); or when the kernel is matmul-dominated (the win is bounded by the vector-stage share). Distinct from `operand-precision-gated-by-consumer-invariance` (that rule SELECTS which precision a stage computes at, licensed by the consumer's tolerance of the wider value; this rule ELIMINATES the wide probability intermediate entirely -- the shifted score never becomes a high-precision probability at all: precision selection vs intermediate elimination), from `coarse-precision-first-exact-refine` (that keeps the exact pass and refines; this never computes the wide pass), from `exp2-log2-base2` (base-2 rewrite still evaluates ONE elementwise exponential per score; this evaluates NONE for the current block -- per element: subtract, multiply, floor, clip), and from `softmax-denominator-cancels-through-scale-invariant-norm` (normalizer ALGEBRA cancellation; the normalizer here still runs in fp and sums the approximated codes -- 'the final output remains a normalized weighted sum'). Source: arXiv 2609.09721 (EFQ-Softmax, walk #9 lead 1) -- Sec. II-B Eqs. 8-10 (the scale + residual rules), Sec. II-C Eq. 11 (the affine code rule + the nearest-rounding trade), Sec. III-A (the shared-operand consistency requirement), Sec. IV-E Table VIII (the vector-stage table; fixpipe + mmad exclusions), Sec. IV-B (the quality table), Sec. V (the scope caveats). Batch 145 / Plan 130 / Research 171.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// NAIVE -- wide-precision probability producer feeding a low-bit matmul:\n// the exp-then-quantize roundtrip (dense exp on every score, then a\n// separate quantize pass over the tile it just built).\nfn p_tile_exp_then_quantize(\n    scores: &mut [f32], m_b: f32, out_codes: &mut [u8], out_scale: &mut f32,\n) {\n    // stage 1: dense elementwise exp (the vector-stage cost)\n    for s in scores.iter_mut() {\n        *s = (*s - m_b).exp(); // (s_b::exp()) -- wide p tile\n    }\n    // stage 2: quantize the tile we JUST materialized (amax + scale + round)\n    let mut amax: f32 = 0.0;\n    for &p in scores.iter() {\n        amax = amax.max(p);\n    }\n    *out_scale = amax / 6.0; // center under E2M1 max code\n    for (c, &p) in out_codes.iter_mut().zip(scores.iter()) {\n        *c = (p / out_scale).round().clamp(0.0, 7.0) as u8;\n    }\n}\n"
                .to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// FIX -- direct generation: shifted score -> code in ONE pass, exponent-only\n// block scale, ONE calibrated affine rule; no wide p tile ever exists.\n// Consistency: the SAME (code, scale) operand feeds numerator AND denominator.\nstruct EfqCalib {\n    tau: f32, // offline, output-level calibrated; fixed at inference\n    h: f32,\n}\n\nfn p_tile_direct_low_bit(\n    scores: &[f32],            // ALREADY row-max-shifted (x_t <= 0)\n    calib: &EfqCalib,\n    out_codes: &mut [u8],\n    out_scale: &mut f32,\n) {\n    // per microscaling block: block max -> exponent-only scale\n    let m_b = scores.iter().copied().fold(f32::NEG_INFINITY, f32::max);\n    let k_b = ((m_b + (2.0f32 / 9.0).ln()) / 2.0f32.ln()).floor();\n    let s_b = 2.0f32.powi(k_b as i32); // 2^k_b <= 2*e^{M_b}/9 < 2^{k_b+1}\n    let log6 = 6.0f32.ln();\n    for (c, &x) in out_codes.iter_mut().zip(scores.iter()) {\n        let z = x - k_b * 2.0f32.ln() - log6;      // residual log domain\n        let raw = ((z - calib.tau) * calib.h).floor() + 1.0;\n        *c = raw.clamp(0.0, 7.0) as u8;            // C_8 index\n    }\n    *out_scale = s_b; // numerator and denominator share (codes, s_b)\n}\n"
                .to_string(),
        },

        // ============================================================
        // -- kv-split-decode-underfill-per-slot-validity -- Batch 145 /
        // Research 171 / Plan 130 (MTPLX lane).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "kv-split-decode-underfill-per-slot-validity".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A decode-attention dispatch whose grid is sized over QUERY ROWS ONLY -- one threadgroup per (row, kv_head), the row-parallel shape -- underfills the GPU at decode M and each surviving threadgroup serially walks the whole selected KV sequence: 'At prefill (4,096 rows) that is 8,192 threadgroups. At M=4 it is EIGHT threadgroups on a 40-core M5 Max, and at M=1 it is TWO -- and each of those few threadgroups still walks all 2,051 selected keys x 256 dims.' Split the REDUCTION axis (the KV sequence) across many threadgroups instead: grid becomes (rows, kv_heads, n_splits) with tid.z the KV split (the batch axis was carrying no information -- the contract already refuses B != 1); each split accumulates an UNNORMALIZED partial online-softmax state (acc, m, l) over its tile slice [t0, t1) with the final O/sum divide MOVED to the merge pass ('the merge pass owns the normalisation, because the denominator is only known once every split has reported'); a second merge kernel per (head, row) does the log-sum-exp combine. THREE load-bearing details beyond the generic split. (1) THE UNDERFILL DUAL-SOURCE (eligibility evidence, the rider): the negative cell is measured (hyper_read v1's row-parallel (1024, S, 1) grid at S=4: '4 threadgroups on a 40-core GPU... Measured on the M4 verifier 2026-09-01: 13.2 tok/s with MTPLX_FUSED_HC=1 vs 67.8 control, every GPU phase ~5x slower because the underfilled kernel backs the queue up' -- the queue-backup mechanism generalizes) and independently confirmed off-Metal (Hopper 2609.12923: bf16 GMMA executes FIXED 64-row fragments, so small-batch decode fills only a fraction of each fragment with real token rows -- the same underfill class read off the fragment geometry). (2) PER-SLOT VALIDITY, NOT A PREFIX CUT, when the KV selection is SPARSE: 'Validity is decided PER SLOT (block_id < complete_blocks), not by a leading-prefix cut... The DECODE branch does not sort: it hands mx.argpartition's raw output straight to the rows-gather token list... Applying the prefix cut to an unsorted row would drop visible blocks and admit invisible ones' -- and the near-miss predicate is a double-count trap: candidate <= q_abs 'would admit the tokens of the incomplete block... and double-count them in the softmax denominator'; invalid slots are masked to -inf in the score tile and skipped (zero-word load) in the K/V stage. (3) NO EMPTY SPLITS: clamp the split count to the tile count and round DOWN -- tiles/split = ceil, then n_splits = ceil(tiles/tiles_per_split) -- 'so dispatching eight would leave two threadgroups writing nothing.' Combine-pass trade: it is a second launch; mitigate by putting both dispatches in ONE encoder ('no host sync between them and Metal's in-encoder ordering is the barrier'). Regime: decode M <= ~8 at long/sparse KV where rows x heads underfills the SM count; prefill M keeps the row-parallel kernel (its grid already fills the machine). Distinct from `split-k-two-pass-attention-decode-long-context` (Batch 105 -- the DENSE long-context twin: the crossover keys on CONTEXT LENGTH (32K+) with a fixed-block shape-gated instantiation and stride-partitioned blocks; THIS keys on grid WIDTH UNDERFILL at decode M -- rows x heads vs SM count, not a length threshold -- and carries the sparse half: unsorted per-slot validity, the <= double-count hazard, and the exact_splits no-empty-split clamp, none of which a dense split carries; the partial-state + LSE-merge mechanism is SHARED and the two rules cross-reference), from `row-dot-split-inner-axis-block-reduce` (splits the reduction INSIDE one dot; this splits the SEQUENCE across threadgroups), from `tiny-rows-scalar-thread-per-row-downshift` (downshifts thread organization BELOW warp width for tiny rows; this MULTIPLIES threadgroups along the reduction axis at fixed lane count), and from `gqa-kv-head-sharing-bandwidth-reduction` (shares K/V across GQA heads for bandwidth; this splits one head's sequence for parallelism). Source: youssofal/MTPLX @ a5e38bb7 (PR #391, Apache-2.0, MLX 0.32.2; kernel provenance in-tree: oMLX jundot steel_qwen4_qsa_sparse_gqa.h <- mlx-serve MIT msv_attn_p256) -- native_extensions/qsa_sparse_gqa/sparse_gqa/steel_qsa_sparse_gqa_decode.h:13-61 (the WHY comment: grid shapes, the per-slot-validity block, the <= hazard), :201-216 + :255-300 (the per-slot predicate + masking + skip), mtplx/kernels/qsa_sparse_decode.py:44-49 (the KV-split dispatch statement), native_extensions/qsa_sparse_gqa/sparse_gqa/qsa_sparse_gqa_decode.cpp:197-200 (one-encoder) + :296-312 (the exact_splits clamp), mtplx/kernels/laguna_sdpa_2pass.py:25-33 (the two-pass contract), mtplx/kernels/qwen4_m4_hyper_read.py:14-20 (the 13.2/67.8 underfill cell), Hopper 2609.12923 (the GMMA fragment-fill confirmation). Batch 145 / Plan 130 / Research 171.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// NAIVE -- row-parallel decode attention: one threadgroup per (row, head),\n// the whole selected KV walked serially inside it. M=4 -> 8 threadgroups on\n// a 40-core part; every SM past the eighth idles while each of the eight\n// walks all 2,051 selected keys x 256 dims (the ~5x phase slowdown shape).\nfn decode_attention_row_parallel(\n    q: &[f32],                  // [rows, d]\n    k_sel: &[f32],              // [rows, n_sel, d] selected keys\n    v_sel: &[f32],\n    valid: &[bool],             // per-slot visibility (UNSORTED selection)\n    out: &mut [f32],            // [rows, d]\n) {\n    let rows = q.len() / 64;\n    for r in 0..rows {\n        // ONE threadgroup owns the whole row: every other SM idle.\n        let mut m = f32::NEG_INFINITY;\n        let mut l = 0.0f32;\n        let mut acc = [0.0f32; 64];\n        for s in 0..2048 {\n            if !valid[s] { continue; }      // prefix cut? argpartition output\n            let mut score = 0.0f32;         // is UNSORTED -- a prefix cut drops\n            for d in 0..64 {                // visible blocks AND admits\n                score += q[r * 64 + d] * k_sel[r * 2048 * 64 + s * 64 + d];\n            }                               // invisible ones\n            let m_new = m.max(score);\n            let alpha = (m - m_new).exp();\n            l = l * alpha + score.exp();\n            for d in 0..64 {\n                acc[d] = acc[d] * alpha + score.exp() * v_sel[r * 2048 * 64 + s * 64 + d];\n            }\n            m = m_new;\n        }\n        for d in 0..64 { out[r * 64 + d] = acc[d] / l; }\n    }\n}\n"
                .to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// FIX -- split the REDUCTION axis: (rows, heads, n_splits) with tid.z the\n// split; per-slot validity (block < complete_blocks, NOT <= and NOT a\n// prefix cut); unnormalized partials + LSE merge that owns the divide;\n// splits clamped + rounded down so none is empty.\nconst N_SPLITS: usize = 16;\n\nstruct Partial { m: f32, l: f32, acc: Vec<f32> }\n\nfn decode_attention_kv_split(\n    q: &[f32], k_sel: &[f32], v_sel: &[f32],\n    selected: &[i64],           // raw argpartition slots (UNSORTED)\n    complete_blocks: i64,       // per-slot predicate bound\n    partials: &mut [Partial],   // [rows * n_splits] scratch\n) {\n    let rows = q.len() / 64;\n    let per_split = (2048 + N_SPLITS - 1) / N_SPLITS;\n    // round DOWN so the last split is never empty\n    let n_splits = (2048 + per_split - 1) / per_split;\n    for r in 0..rows {\n        for sp in 0..n_splits {\n            let t0 = sp * per_split;\n            let t1 = 2048.min(t0 + per_split);\n            let p = &mut partials[r * n_splits + sp];\n            p.m = f32::NEG_INFINITY;\n            p.l = 0.0;\n            p.acc = vec![0.0; 64];\n            for s in t0..t1 {\n                // PER SLOT, not a prefix cut: the decode selector does not sort.\n                if selected[s] < 0 || selected[s] >= complete_blocks { continue; }\n                let mut score = 0.0f32;\n                for d in 0..64 {\n                    score += q[r * 64 + d] * k_sel[r * 2048 * 64 + s * 64 + d];\n                }\n                let m_new = p.m.max(score);\n                let alpha = (p.m - m_new).exp();\n                p.l = p.l * alpha + score.exp();\n                for d in 0..64 {\n                    p.acc[d] = p.acc[d] * alpha + score.exp() * v_sel[r * 2048 * 64 + s * 64 + d];\n                }\n                p.m = m_new;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // merge pass (same encoder, no host sync): LSE combine owns the divide.\n    for r in 0..rows {\n        let mut m_g = f32::NEG_INFINITY;\n        for sp in 0..n_splits { m_g = m_g.max(partials[r * n_splits + sp].m); }\n        let mut l_g = 0.0f32;\n        let mut out = [0.0f32; 64];\n        for sp in 0..n_splits {\n            let p = &partials[r * n_splits + sp];\n            let w = ((p.m - m_g).exp()) * p.l; // rescale into the global max\n            l_g += w;\n            for d in 0..64 { out[d] += p.acc[d] * (p.m - m_g).exp(); }\n        }\n        for d in 0..64 { out[d] /= l_g; } // the merge owns the normalisation\n    }\n}\n"
                .to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
